  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 207 PM EST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nIn the upper levels a sharp trough continues to swing through the \nGreat Lakes and Ohio Valley region. An associated cold front is \nprogressing through the locale with some light rain/drizzle in wake, \nall while the healthier band of precipitation has long since \ndissipated. Latest RAP soundings depict moisture shallows out \nthroughout the afternoon and evening, allowing rain/drizzle chances \nto gradually come to an end followed by decreasing clouds tonight. \nOne thing to note as we cross into the AM hours is that an expanding \narea of high pressure to our northwest and CAD on the eastern side \nof the mountains will strengthen the pressure gradient locally. \nBecause of this, some breezy winds are expected through Tuesday\nmorning. Latest REFS paint much of the valley with some 20-30mph \ngusts, locally up to 35. Nudged up the overnight lows a tad from \nthe NBM as the wind should help keep the boundary layer from \ndecoupling. \n\nWinds will lessen along side dry conditions as we head into the \nafternoon. An additional trough dives south across the Plains \nstarting Tuesday night. This will bring a return of rain chances \nWednesday into Thanksgiving as strong surface cyclogenesis is \nexpected to occur as the system lifts north and eastward. In terms\nof rainfall, this will be largely beneficial rain. Most QPF \namounts are generally between 0.75-1.5\", though, locally higher \ntotals cannot be ruled out in favored upslope locations. \n\nThe main hazard will be the potential for strong winds across the \nTennessee mountains, however, there is still a bit of uncertainty \nhow the system will evolve. Overall, latest trends in guidance have \nstrengthened a cross-mountain low level jet Wednesday afternoon \nthrough Thanksgiving morning relative to previous runs. Based on \nREFS probabilities, went with wording for up to 50 mph gusts in \nHWO text, but if run-to-run consistency is exhibited with recent \nupticks in LLJ amplitude, that may be too low. Some occasional \nadvisory criteria gusts could start early Wednesday morning, but \nmore notably increasing by the afternoon and continue through AM \nhours for Thanksgiving day. Strongest gusty winds in the mountains\nwill decrease into the afternoon but as the surface pressure \ngradient increases in the evening, breezy winds will develop area \nwide. \n\nSteady moderate rain will diminish Thanksgiving night, however, \nwrap around moisture will continue to promote some light precip \ninto Friday. Some of this may fall as snow and lead to light \nsnowfall accumulations across higher elevations. Temperatures will\nbe cool for Thursday and Friday, with a brief rebound to near \nnormal temperatures Saturday. Dry conditions are expected \nSaturday before an additional system is possible late weekend into\nearly next week.",
